---
/**
 * Section 01 — what a quantum causal model is, and where the crate draws the
 * line between structure and state.
 *
 * The figure carries the load here. The prose says only what the picture
 * cannot: that the factors are freeze-time decoration rather than runtime
 * state, which is the design decision a reader has to accept before any of the
 * rest makes sense.
 */
import SupportsDiagram from './SupportsDiagram.astro';
---
<section class="qsec page" data-rise>
  <header class="sec-head">
    <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-coord">
      <span class="ix">01 / 05</span><span class="lb">Model</span>
    </p>
    <h2>Channels where the conditional tables used to be</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      A classical causal model factorizes a joint distribution over its graph. A
      quantum causal model does the same for a process operator.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="body-cols">
    <div>
      <p class="body">
        Every node <code>Aᵢ</code> carries a Choi–Jamiołkowski operator
        <code>ρ_&#123;Aᵢ|Pa(Aᵢ)&#125;</code>, and the model is their product
        <code>σ = ∏ᵢ ρ_&#123;Aᵢ|Pa(Aᵢ)&#125;</code>. That product is the process
        operator, and it lives on the composite Hilbert space of everything the
        graph touches.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        A product of operators is not automatically a model. Lorenz (2022,
        Def. 3.3) states the condition it has to meet: factors whose Hilbert
        supports intersect must pairwise commute. Classical conditionals commute
        for free, which is why the classical case never has to say this out loud.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="body">
        The factors ride an external <code>ProcessFactors</code> decoration,
        keyed by graph node index, with a separate <code>FactorSupports</code>
        recording which legs each one lives on. They are static freeze-time
        data. They never travel on the runtime state channel, which is what
        makes the condition checkable once instead of per step.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        <code>FactorSupports::from_graph</code> derives the supports from the
        graph rather than asking the caller to restate them, so the two cannot
        drift apart.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <SupportsDiagram />

  <p class="more">
    <a href="/qcm/">The model in full, including faithfulness</a>
    <a href="/operators/">The operator layer it is built from</a>
  </p>
</section>

<style>
  .qsec { max-width: var(--w-panel); }
  .sec-head { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .sec-head h2 { margin: var(--space-3) 0 var(--space-3); }
  .lede { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: var(--measure-lede); margin: 0; }

  .body-cols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-5); }
  @media (min-width: 900px) { .body-cols { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-7); } }

  .body { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); }
  .body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .more {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1);
    font-size: var(--t-body-sm);
  }
</style>
